# nodus/tuvm/tests/target/wedge-quiet.env — rete#215, design §8 row 5
#
# THE FALSE-TRIP GATE. §8 names a false trip as "the failure mode that would make this
# instrument worse than nothing", and this is the only scenario that can see it. The node
# declares one 10-minute timer, says so once, and must still be on its FIRST BOOT when the
# window closes.
#
# OBSERVE_PROGRESS IS `none`, AND THAT IS THE CORRECT ANSWER HERE RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
# collect::observe requires a progress line every OBSERVE_POLL_S (10 s by default), and a
# node whose only timer is ten minutes long has nothing to print in between. Giving it a
# fast heartbeat would mean DECLARING a fast repeating timer — which is exactly the state
# this scenario exists to prove the detector does not assume, so the heartbeat would
# quietly convert this into a test of nothing.
#
# What carries the row instead is the runner's FAULT SCAN, whose pattern already includes
# `rst:0x` — the ESP32 ROM's reset line. A false trip reboots the node, a reboot prints
# `rst:0x`, and the scan fails the window. That is a complete assertion for the failure
# this row is about.
#
# WHAT THIS ROW CANNOT SEE, said out loud: a SILENT death of the slow node inside the
# window. There is no positive predicate to miss it with. That failure belongs to
# wedge-detect and to the armed soak, not here.
#
# 600 s is chosen against the DETECTOR, not for feel: the observer's own backstop is 30 s
# and every fixed deadline it could plausibly have carried is smaller still, so a node
# alive at ten minutes with nothing but a 10-minute timer has demonstrably been judged
# against 600000 ms. Shorter, and the gate would pass for a detector with a 5-minute
# constant in it.
#
# class=disruptive: it holds the board for ten minutes doing nothing. Nothing here writes,
# reboots or latches, but it is not something to point at the bell.
SCENARIO=wedge-quiet
NODE=SlowTimerNodeC6.nsl
BOARD_ID=esp32c6
CLASS=disruptive
TIMEOUT_S=120

OBSERVE_S=600
OBSERVE_PROGRESS=none
